1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,057
Welcome to lectures nineteen and twenty:

2
00:00:03,057 --> 00:00:03,745
outliers.

3
00:00:03,745 --> 00:00:09,096
What they are, why they happen, how to find them, and how to fix them.

4
00:00:09,896 --> 00:00:11,570
Here is the session map.

5
00:00:11,570 --> 00:00:27,470
The lecture covers what an outlier is, why outliers occur, four types, how to find them with box plots, Z-scores, I Q R and machine learning models, why outliers spoil results, how to fix them, and what happens if we do nothing.

6
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:34,304
The practical applies all of this to New York City Airbnb data and repeats it on a second dataset.

7
00:00:35,104 --> 00:00:40,699
An outlier is a value that is very far from the other values in the same column.

8
00:00:40,699 --> 00:00:43,496
It is unusual, but not always a mistake.

9
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:44,055
Example:

10
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:51,608
seven staff earn thirty to forty five thousand rupees a month, and the director earns five hundred thousand.

11
00:00:51,608 --> 00:00:56,713
The mean is ninety five thousand, but the median is thirty nine thousand.

12
00:00:56,713 --> 00:01:02,518
One person pushed the mean up two and a half times while the median hardly changed.

13
00:01:02,518 --> 00:01:08,392
Mean and standard deviation are easily pulled by outliers; median and I Q R are not.

14
00:01:09,192 --> 00:01:14,645
In the real Airbnb data, in most columns the mean is much bigger than the median.

15
00:01:14,645 --> 00:01:25,551
Price has a mean of one fifty three but a median of one oh six, with a maximum of ten thousand dollars per night, while a typical home costs only one hundred six.

16
00:01:25,551 --> 00:01:30,264
Minimum nights, reviews and host listing counts show the same pattern:

17
00:01:30,264 --> 00:01:33,024
a few very large values pull the mean up.

18
00:01:33,824 --> 00:01:34,678
Four causes.

19
00:01:34,678 --> 00:01:36,030
A data-entry error:

20
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:43,646
eleven homes with a price of zero, and fourteen homes asking for more than three hundred sixty five nights.

21
00:01:43,646 --> 00:01:44,927
A genuine extreme:

22
00:01:44,927 --> 00:01:51,332
two hundred thirty nine luxury homes above a thousand dollars a night, real but very rare.

23
00:01:51,332 --> 00:01:52,613
A measurement cap:

24
00:01:52,613 --> 00:02:02,648
nine hundred sixty five California districts stored at exactly five hundred thousand and one, where the value was stopped at a maximum limit.

25
00:02:02,648 --> 00:02:04,570
And a different population:

26
00:02:04,570 --> 00:02:09,908
one host owns three hundred twenty seven homes, a company and not a person.

27
00:02:09,908 --> 00:02:10,904
First ask why.

28
00:02:11,704 --> 00:02:13,109
There are four types.

29
00:02:13,109 --> 00:02:19,332
Global outliers are very far from all other values, like three homes at ten thousand dollars.

30
00:02:19,332 --> 00:02:26,894
Contextual outliers are normal overall but odd for their group, like two hundred fifty dollars for a shared room.

31
00:02:26,894 --> 00:02:35,125
Collective outliers are each normal but odd as a group, like one host listing forty eight rooms at exactly two thirty nine.

32
00:02:35,125 --> 00:02:44,560
And multivariate outliers have each column normal but the combination odd, like eleven point eight reviews a month but only fifteen in total.

33
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,906
Contextual outliers need each group checked separately.

34
00:02:48,906 --> 00:03:01,350
The upper fence is three hundred ninety two dollars for entire homes, only one sixty two for private rooms, and one thirty eight for shared rooms, versus three thirty four for the whole column.

35
00:03:01,350 --> 00:03:08,765
One thousand twenty one homes are too costly for their room type but look normal when all rooms are mixed together.

36
00:03:08,765 --> 00:03:13,536
One limit for everyone misses them; a separate limit per group finds them.

37
00:03:14,336 --> 00:03:16,500
Three ways to find outliers.

38
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:17,736
Look at a chart:

39
00:03:17,736 --> 00:03:22,991
a box plot, a histogram or a scatter plot, and always do this first.

40
00:03:22,991 --> 00:03:24,382
Use a simple rule:

41
00:03:24,382 --> 00:03:32,496
Z-score greater than three, the I Q R fences, or the modified Z-score, which checks one column at a time.

42
00:03:32,496 --> 00:03:42,310
Or use a machine learning model, like Isolation Forest, Local Outlier Factor or D B S C A N, which check many columns together.

43
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:47,024
Start with a chart, then a rule, then a model if you need it.

44
00:03:47,824 --> 00:03:50,414
The box plot finds outliers by eye.

45
00:03:50,414 --> 00:04:01,809
The box holds the middle fifty percent of values, the red line is the median, whiskers show the normal range, and the orange dots are outliers outside it.

46
00:04:01,809 --> 00:04:05,434
Five of six columns have many dots above the box.

47
00:04:05,434 --> 00:04:07,432
Only availability has none.

48
00:04:08,232 --> 00:04:16,476
The Z-score is the value minus the mean over the standard deviation, the number of standard deviations from the mean.

49
00:04:16,476 --> 00:04:20,633
If it is above three in absolute value, call it an outlier.

50
00:04:20,633 --> 00:04:29,299
Use it when data looks like a bell curve; avoid it when data has a long tail on one side, because outliers hide themselves.

51
00:04:29,299 --> 00:04:39,163
For price, ten thousand dollars gives a Z of forty one, an outlier, but five hundred dollars gives only one point four five, not an outlier.

52
00:04:39,163 --> 00:04:43,109
The Z limit is only eight hundred seventy three dollars.

53
00:04:43,109 --> 00:04:48,816
A safer version uses the median, the modified Z, flagging above three point five.

54
00:04:49,616 --> 00:04:52,616
The I Q R rule uses the box plot fences.

55
00:04:52,616 --> 00:04:58,992
Q one is sixty nine and Q three is one seventy five, so the I Q R is one hundred six.

56
00:04:58,992 --> 00:05:05,667
The upper fence is Q three plus one point five I Q R, which is three thirty four dollars.

57
00:05:05,667 --> 00:05:10,168
The lower fence is below zero, so there are no low outliers.

58
00:05:10,168 --> 00:05:15,343
Big values do not move Q one and Q three, so this rule is not fooled.

59
00:05:15,343 --> 00:05:23,144
It flags two thousand nine hundred seventy two price outliers, almost eight times more than the Z-score.

60
00:05:23,944 --> 00:05:27,633
Comparing Z-score and I Q R on all six features:

61
00:05:27,633 --> 00:05:34,320
every column has a long right tail, and Z-score always finds fewer outliers than I Q R.

62
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:41,313
For minimum nights, I Q R flags over six thousand, Z-score only three hundred twenty seven.

63
00:05:41,313 --> 00:05:43,696
On long-tailed data, use I Q R.

64
00:05:44,496 --> 00:05:46,783
Why does Z-score miss outliers?

65
00:05:46,783 --> 00:05:47,963
This is masking.

66
00:05:47,963 --> 00:05:53,495
Three ten thousand dollar homes make the standard deviation big, two forty.

67
00:05:53,495 --> 00:05:58,659
A big standard deviation pushes the Z limit up to eight seventy three.

68
00:05:58,659 --> 00:06:03,380
The outliers stretch the ruler that measures them, so they hide.

69
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:07,585
Q one and Q three do not stretch, so I Q R is not fooled.

70
00:06:07,585 --> 00:06:18,502
Two thousand five hundred eighty four homes priced between three thirty four and eight seventy three are outliers by I Q R but invisible to Z-score.

71
00:06:18,502 --> 00:06:19,387
Simple rule:

72
00:06:19,387 --> 00:06:23,592
Z-score for bell-shaped data, I Q R for long-tailed data.

73
00:06:24,392 --> 00:06:27,587
Three models find outliers using many columns.

74
00:06:27,587 --> 00:06:37,170
Isolation Forest keeps cutting the data at random, and odd points get separated very quickly; you set the percentage of outliers expected.

75
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:44,393
Local Outlier Factor flags a point far more alone than its neighbours; you set the number of neighbours.

76
00:06:44,393 --> 00:06:51,824
D B S C A N makes clusters, and points that fit no group are outliers; you set the group distance and size.

77
00:06:52,624 --> 00:06:53,763
Compare them.

78
00:06:53,763 --> 00:07:01,734
Z-score and I Q R look at one column; Isolation Forest, L O F and D B S C A N look at many.

79
00:07:01,734 --> 00:07:06,377
Isolation Forest always flags the percentage you set.

80
00:07:06,377 --> 00:07:08,479
L O F needs scaled data.

81
00:07:08,479 --> 00:07:12,070
D B S C A N changes with its eps setting.

82
00:07:12,070 --> 00:07:18,553
All three models give minus one for an outlier and one for a normal point.

83
00:07:18,553 --> 00:07:19,516
One column:

84
00:07:19,516 --> 00:07:20,480
use a rule.

85
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:22,845
An odd mix of many columns:

86
00:07:22,845 --> 00:07:23,896
use a model.

87
00:07:24,696 --> 00:07:26,324
If we ignore outliers:

88
00:07:26,324 --> 00:07:33,502
one, the mean is wrong; the mean price of one fifty three is forty four percent above the median.

89
00:07:33,502 --> 00:07:41,495
Two, the spread looks too big; the standard deviation is two forty before and only eighty four after fixing.

90
00:07:41,495 --> 00:07:47,711
Three, very lopsided data; a skew of nineteen breaks tests that expect a bell curve.

91
00:07:47,711 --> 00:07:52,225
Four, a regression line gets pulled; one bad row can flip it.

92
00:07:52,225 --> 00:07:58,738
Five, distance-based methods like K N N and K-Means are controlled by a few huge values.

93
00:07:58,738 --> 00:08:04,584
Six, error scores like M S E square the errors, so a few rows decide the score.

94
00:08:05,384 --> 00:08:06,729
Nine ways to fix.

95
00:08:06,729 --> 00:08:09,578
Remove the row, for a clear mistake.

96
00:08:09,578 --> 00:08:14,642
Cap at the I Q R fence, to keep every row but shrink big values.

97
00:08:14,642 --> 00:08:18,124
Winsorise at the top and bottom one percent.

98
00:08:18,124 --> 00:08:23,188
Replace with the median, or the group median when groups differ.

99
00:08:23,188 --> 00:08:29,914
Replace using K N N or regression when similar rows or other columns predict it well.

100
00:08:29,914 --> 00:08:34,424
Take a log of one plus x for real but very spread values.

101
00:08:34,424 --> 00:08:40,517
Or flag with a zero-one column when the outlier itself is useful information.

102
00:08:40,517 --> 00:08:42,416
Only removal loses rows.

103
00:08:43,216 --> 00:08:43,981
Which fix?

104
00:08:43,981 --> 00:08:45,051
First ask why.

105
00:08:45,051 --> 00:08:48,874
A price of zero or minimum nights of twelve fifty:

106
00:08:48,874 --> 00:08:50,097
is it a mistake?

107
00:08:50,097 --> 00:08:54,761
Correct it if you can, otherwise remove it or use the median.

108
00:08:54,761 --> 00:08:57,742
A ten thousand dollar luxury penthouse:

109
00:08:57,742 --> 00:08:58,583
is it real?

110
00:08:58,583 --> 00:09:03,782
Keep it, but cap it or use the log so it does not control the model.

111
00:09:03,782 --> 00:09:07,911
A house value stored at five hundred thousand and one:

112
00:09:07,911 --> 00:09:08,981
is it a limit?

113
00:09:08,981 --> 00:09:14,180
Mark it with a flag or drop it, since we do not know the true value.

114
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:18,232
Do not delete a value just because a rule flagged it.

